/*! \page developer_backend_page Backend Identifier Classes

\ref developer_containers_page (Prev/Up)  &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;
\ref developer_param_page      (Next)
<BR>

The PSCF package builds two executable files, named pscf_rpc and pscf_rpg,
that are variants of the same program, but that are specialized to use 
different hardware. The pscf_rpc executable uses only standard CPU 
hardware, and is constructed using only standard C++ source code.  
The pscf_rpg executable instead makes use of an NVIDIA GPU for some
operations, and built using some CUDA C++ code.  

The source code from which these two executables are built differs 
primarily in the treatment of a few basic mathematical operations that 
are performed on large arrays. Among these are elementwise linear algebra 
operations, array reductions such as inner products, and fast Fourier 
transform (FFT) operations.  In code that is designed to use a GPU, these 
basic operations are performed on the GPU, by kernel functions that are 
coded in CUDA C++.  In analogous code that is designed for CPU hardware, 
these mathematical operations are performed on the CPU, by functions 
that are written in standard C++.  The use of different hardware for 
computation also affects where data is stored: PSCF stores key data 
structures such as the arrays that contain the w-fields and c-fields 
in GPU global memory in code that uses a GPU, but in in CPU main memory 
in code that uses a CPU. We describe these differences in what follows 
by saying that analogous executable files use different computational 
"backends" for core computations.

\section backend_id_class_sec Identifier Classes

PSCF relies on the use templates to define closely analogous classes 
that use different computational backends. All classes and functions 
that must be specialized to allow the use of more than one possible 
backend are defined as templates that accept a special type of template
parameter that is used to specify the choice of backend. 

PSCF defines two classes named Pscf:CPT and Pscf::CUT that are designed 
to be used as template parameter arguments in order to specify a choice
of computational backend:
  - Use of the class Pscf::CUT in this role indicates a template
    specialization that uses a CUDA backend, which uses a GPU.
  - Use of the class Pscf::CPT in this role indicates a template 
    specialization that uses a C++ backend, on uses only a CPU.

We refer to these two classes as backend identifier classes.  A template 
parameter that is designed to take one of the backend identifier classes
as its argument is referredt to a a backend identifier template 
parameter, or simply a backend identifier parameter  By convention, a
backend identifier parameter is always denoted by the symbol "T" in any
template definition.

Many of the class templates that accept a backend identifier template
parameter also take the integer dimension of spaced, denoted by D,
as an additional parameter. In such templates, the integer parameter 
D is always listed as the first parameter, and backend template parameter
T as a second.  Almost all of the class templates defined in namespace 
Pscf::Rp follow this pattern. 

For example, the Pscf::Rp::System class template, which represents
the highest level object in a SCFT or PS-SCFT calculation, is declared 
within the Pscf::Rp namespace by a template with an signature
\code
    template <int D, class T> class System;
\endcode
An instantiation of this template must use an integer argument D=1, 2, 
or 3 for the int parameter D, and one of the template identifier classes 
as the argument for the class parameter T.  In code that is defined 
within the Pscf::Rp namespace, System \<3, CUT\> is thus the name of
a specialization of the System template that uses a CUDA GPU backend 
to simulate a physical model that uses 3-dimensional periodic fields. 

\section developer_periodic_field_sec Field and FFT classes

The most basic data structures used by PSCF programs for periodic 
systems are arrays that store periodic fields (i.e., functions of 
position) that are defined on the nodes of a regular periodic mesh.
The PSCF package defines a set of class templates in namespace 
Pscf::Prdc that represent such fields and Fourier transforms of 
such fields.  This namespace also contains a definition of a 
template for a class named Prdc::FFT that performs forward and
inverse fast Fourier transform on such fields.  All of these 
field and FFT templates are defined using the template pattern 
described above. 

For example, the class template RField represents a real-valued 
field defined on the nodes of periodic mesh. It is defined in 
namespace Pscf::Prdc as template with a declaration 
\code
  template <int D, class T> class RField;
\endcode
A specialization RField \<3, CUT\> thus represents a real field 
defined on a 3-dimensional periodic mesh that is designed to allow 
use of a GPU, while RField \<2, CPT\> represents a 2D real field 
that is designed to use only CPU hardware.

The Pscf::Prdc namespace defines four basic class templates named 
RField, RFieldDft, CField, and FFT to store and manipulate periodic 
fields. Each of these four class templates takes the dimension of 
space, D, and a backend parameter T as template parameters. These
classes are:

   - An RField \<D, T\> object contains values of a real-valued 
     periodic field on the nodes of a regular periodic D-dimensional 
     mesh.

   - An RFieldDft \<D, T\> object contains values of the complex-valued 
     discrete Fourier transform (DFT) of a real-valued periodic field.

   - A CField \<D, T\> can contain values of complex-valued field on
     the nodes of a D-dimensional periodic mesh, or the DFT of such
     a field. 

   - FFT \<D,T\> is a wrapper around a fast Fourier transform library
     that can transform a RField \<D,T\> to or from an RFieldDft \<D,T\>, 
     or that can apply a forward or inverse complex-to-complex Fourier 
     transform to a CField \<D, T\> object.

Each of the field classes stores information about the dimensions of 
the mesh on which the field is defined, which is stored as an instance 
of IntVec \<D\>. An instance of FFT \<D,T\> with a specific pair of 
arguments for D and T provides functions that take specializations 
of the field class templates  with the same two template arguments.  

The field and FFT classes described above are actually implemented
by defining completely separate partial specializations for different 
values of the backend parameter T.  The directory src/prdc/cuda 
contains a set of partial specializations of these classes for a 
template argument T=CUT, which are written in CUDA C++ and designed
to use a GPU. The directory src/prdc/cpu contains an analogous set of 
partial specializations for specializations with a template argument 
T=CPU, which are written in standard C++ and designed to use standard 
CPU hardware.  

For example, the partial specialization of RField \<D,T\> for T=CPT is
defined in the file prdc/field/cpu/RField.h as a template
\code
    template <int D>
    class RField<D, CPT> : public FftwDArray<double>
    {
      ....
    }
\endcode
Each specializations of this template with D=1, 2, or 3 is a subclass
subclasses of FftwDArray \<double\>, which is a dynamically allocated 
array of double precision numbers with a memory alignment designed 
to optimize performance of the FFTW. The class FftwDArray \<double\> is,
in turn, a subclass of Array \<double\>, which defines an interface that
allows access to individual elements using a square-bracket subscript
operator.

Partial specializations of the field class templates RField, RFieldDft
and CField that are defined to use different backends use blocks of 
memory that are allocated in different locations: Specializations of 
these templates with T=CPT, which use a C++ backend, allocate underlying 
arrays in CPU main memory.  Corresponding specializations with T=CUT 
instead store field data in global CPU memory in order to avoid minimize 
the need for operations that copy data between GPU and CPU memory.  As 
a result of this difference, specializations of the field classes with 
T=CUT allow access to individual elements only within CUDA kernels, 
not in C++ code that is executed on the host CPU. Specializations with 
T=CPT instead allow access to individual elements in C++ code via a 
conventional subscript operator.

The partial specializations of the template FFT \<D,T\> for arguments
T=CPT and T=CUT are wrappers around different underlying FFT libraries. 
An instance of FFT \<D, CPT\> with D=1,2, or 3 is a wrapper for the 
open-source FFTW library, which runs on standard CPU hardware.  
A corresponding instance of Pscf::Cuda::FFT \<D, CUT\> is instead a 
wrapper around the NVIDIA cuFFT library, which performs FFT 
operations on a GPU. These two specializations provide closely
analogous interfaces for D-dimensional real-to-complex, 
complex-to-real, and complex-to-complex transforms.  

\section developer_periodic_complex_sec Complex number types

The different FFT libraries used by pscf_rpc and pscf_rpg use 
different, incompatible data types to represent complex numbers.

The FFTW library uses a type named fftw_complex, that is defined in 
the "fftw3.h" FFTW header file. This typename is an alias for a 
two-element array, type double[2], in which the real and imaginary 
parts are stored in elements 0 and 1, respectively.  

The cuFFT library instead uses a struct to represent complex numbers, 
in which the real and imaginary parts are stored as member variables 
named x and y, respectively.  The struct type used by cuFFT for this 
purpose is referred to throughout the source code for PSCF by an 
alias named "cudaComplex", which defined in the file 
src/pscf/cuda/cudaTypes.h.  

To accomodate these differences, partial specializations of the 
RFieldDft and CField each use the same data type to represent an
individual complex number as that used in the associated FFT library. 
The partial specializations RFieldDft \<D,T\> and CField \<3,T\> with 
T=CPUT are thus wrappers around bare arrays of fftw_complex elements 
that are allocated in CPU memory, for compatability with the FFTW 
library. The corresponding specializations with T=CUT are instead 
wrappers around arrays of cudaComplex elements that are allocated 
in global GPU memory, for compatability with the cuFFT library.

To facilitate creation of template code that uses these different types, 
the classes Pscf::CPT and Pscf::CUT each also define a pair of type 
aliases named Real and Complex that are aliases for the data types 
used to represent real and complex number in code that uses a 
particular backend.

\section developer_periodic_programs_sec Rp namespace

The Rp namespace contains a set of class templates that are used to
construct the pscf_rpc and pscf_rpg executables. Every class template
in this namespaces takes the dimension of space, D, and a backend
parameter, T, as template parameters.  The pscf_rpc executable is
constructed using a set of specializations of these templates that
all have a backend template argument T=CPT, while pscf_rpg is built 
using specializations with T=CUT.

Each of executable programs constructed by the PSCF package is 
designed using a hierarchical system of object ownership. Almost every 
object used in a PSCF program is a "child" that is owned by an instance 
of some higher-level "parent" class.  In this system, each "child" object 
is either a member variable of its parent, or is a dynamically allocated 
object that is accessed through a pointer that is a member of the parent 
object, which the parent is responsible for creating and destroying the 
child.  The top of this ownership hierarchy for pscf_rpc and pscf_rpc is 
a class template named System \<D,T\> that directly or indirectly owns 
every object used by the program.  

In this design, the same arguments for the parameters D and T are used 
through this ownership hierarchy: 
An object that is an instantiation of a class template with arguments
arguments D and T is generally owned by a parent that is an instantiation 
of another template with the same two values for these two parameter, 
and may own children that are also template instantiation with these 
two template arguments. An instance of System \<D,T\> with a specific
choice of values for the template parameters (e.g., D=3 and T=CUT) thus
directly or indirectly a set of other objects that are all instances of
class specializations that are use the same value for the dimension of 
space and use the same backend. In particular, a System \<D,T\> object
indirectly owns a set of RField \<D,T\> objects that are used to 
represent chemical potential and monomer concentration fields. 

When PSCF is compiled, instantiation of template specializations in
the Rp namespaces that use a particular backend is controlled by a 
system of makefile variables and associated preprocessor macros:
GPU-enabled specializations with T=CUT are instantiated (i.e., compiled) 
for  possible values of D=1,2 and 3 if and only if the preprocessor 
macro PSCF_CUDA is defined.  Specializations with T=CPT and D=1,2, 
and 3 are instantiated if and only if the macro PSCF_CPP is defined.

